    "Kurt Mitman"
   ],
   "contribution": "Shows that perfect-foresight transition paths after one-time (MIT) shocks can be treated as linearized impulse responses. This gives a fast way to simulate heterogeneous-agent models with aggregate risk, and it influenced sequence-space methods.",
   "confidence": "high"
  }
 ],
 "persona": {
  "summary": "A quantitative macroeconomist and growth theorist at the IIES. His work builds tractable, theory-disciplined models that confront long-run stylized facts: structural change, falling hours, and the productivity slowdown. Much of it is done in close collaboration with Per Krusell. He serves as a co-opted member, so he would likely contribute specific expertise in macroeconomics and growth rather than act as a generalist.",
  "research_lens": "Long-run, general-equilibrium thinking. He asks whether a mechanism is consistent with the Kaldor-type and Kuznets-type facts at once, and whether preferences and technology are specified in a way that aggregates cleanly. He pays close attention to the restrictions that balanced growth places on theory, as in his work on preferences consistent with balanced growth.",
  "methods_and_evidence": "Theory combined with calibration and quantitative structural models. His evidence is long historical time series (sectoral spending shares, hours worked, productivity) and household expenditure data. He also contributes computational methods for heterogeneous-agent macro. His record shows little reliance on randomized or quasi-experimental designs. This is an inference from his publications, not a stated position.",
  "what_they_value_in_contributions": "Inferred from his own work: theoretical frameworks that are elegant and tractable and that reconcile several robust empirical regularities; methods that make previously intractable quantitative questions solvable; and contributions that reshape how the profession models growth, demand, and labor supply over the long run. He would plausibly value durable conceptual tools over isolated empirical findings, while still insisting that the tools be disciplined by data.",
  "likely_questions_in_deliberation": [
   "Does this framework stay consistent with long-run aggregate facts such as balanced growth and stable factor shares, or does it explain one fact at the expense of others?",
   "How general and tractable is the contribution: has it become a standard tool that others build on in quantitative work?",
   "Do the mechanisms survive in general equilibrium, beyond partial-equilibrium or reduced-form estimates?",
   "How much of the observed phenomenon (e.g., the productivity slowdown or structural change) does the work quantitatively account for?",
   "Is the empirical identification credible, and does it speak to aggregate rather than only local effects?"
  ],
  "committee_experience": "Co-opted member of the Committee for the Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel, per nobelprize.org for 2026. I am not certain of his start year, though I believe it is recent (around the mid-2020s). He is part of the IIES group that has long supplied committee members, such as Per Krusell, John Hassler and Tore Ellingsen-era colleagues. Committee deliberations are confidential, and I know of no public statements by him on specific prize decisions."
 },
 "knowledge_limits": "My knowledge runs to roughly 2024-2025 and comes from memory without verification. I am confident about his Econometrica 2014, JPE 2020 and JEDC 2018 papers. I am less sure of exact titles, venues and coauthors for his productivity-slowdown, COVID-19, and directed-technical-change work, so I left those out of the works list. I do not know his exact committee start date, any other editorial or professional service roles, or any working papers from 2025-2026. I have no information on his views about specific prize candidates."
}

